30322265625, 0.0005426406860351562, 0.01105499267578125, -0.0261993408203125, -0.006923675537109375, 0.0228271484375, 0.0057525634765625, 0.005588531494140625, -0.01372528076171875, 0.0313720703125, 0.047637939453125, 0.0175018310546875, 0.0093994140625, 0.01425933837890625, -0.0236358642578125, 0.01031494140625, 0.041046142578125, -0.046112060546875, -0.004180908203125, -0.049041748046875, -0.0506591796875, 0.004657745361328125, -0.005062103271484375, -0.005340576171875, -0.0031032562255859375, 0.017181396484375, -0.01285552978515625, 0.006290435791015625, -0.00547027587890625, -0.01580810546875, -0.0208740234375, -0.0017309188842773438, 0.005474090576171875, 0.01378631591796875, -0.01142120361328125, 0.01218414306640625, -0.006099700927734375, 0.0301971435546875, -0.0105743408203125, 0.01065826416015625, 0.044586181640625, -0.0033111572265625, 0.02197265625, -0.0251007080078125, 0.006336212158203125, 0.0210113525390625, -0.006053924560546875, -0.00751495361328125, 0.01073455810546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is critical for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance, yet it remains a complex task requiring nuanced accounting judgement. Our study addresses the challenge of improving automated invoice categorisation for businesses with unique accounting contexts, where standard AI solutions may fall short. Specifically, we investigate whether small in-house language models (SLMs) can offer competitive accuracy while enhancing data security and interpretability compared to larger, cloud-based models.\n\nTo this end, we compare two pre-trained models\u2014SBERT (sentence transformer) and DeBERTa (classic SLM)\u2014for their ability to represent the financial text in a specialised corpus. We find that sentence embeddings in this setting are \"globally anisotropic but locally isotropic\": while the overall embedding space is unevenly distributed (anisotropic), invoices from the same vendor form tightly packed, uniform clusters (locally isotropic), suggesting vendor identity strongly influences classification.\n\nSBERT, fine-tuned on a single GPU, achieves a 0.96 accuracy rate on a held-out test set of 1,000 invoices from diverse clients and vendors, averaged over five cross-validation folds. This performance surpasses both a zero-shot large language model (LLM), which had no domain-specific training, and a simple vendor-based classification baseline. The comparison demonstrates the value of fine-tuning smaller models specifically on domain-relevant data. Notably, SBERT improves accuracy for infrequent GL codes and performs robustly for new client data\u2014achieving a 0.9 F1 score after training with around 100 client-specific invoices.\n\nOur analysis further reveals that regions of the embedding space with well-formed clusters correspond to higher classification accuracy, concretely linking embedding geometry to performance. Surprisingly, formatting invoice text into structured fields (e.g., line items, descriptions, totals), while beneficial for human readers, does not enhance SLM classification results\u2014suggesting the model effectively extracts relevant features from less structured input.\n\nNonetheless, our study is limited by the size of the available labelled dataset and the specificity to certain business domains, which may restrict generalisability. Future work should explore broader financial corpora, larger-scale evaluations, and adaptive methods to handle evolving business requirements.\n\nIn summary, our findings suggest that small in-house SLMs, when carefully fine-tuned, can outperform both off-the-shelf LLMs and simple heuristics for invoice categorisation, offering a practical and secure alternative for organisations seeking tailored AI solutions."}}